From this single mechanism, Reb Nosson derives: why livelihood is &ldquo;as difficult as the Splitting of the Sea&rdquo; (same mechanism — the matteh oz forced the Sea to split and Egypt to disgorge); why we mention g&rsquo;vuros g&rsquo;shamim (mighty acts of rain) specifically in the blessing of Attah Gibbor (rain = livelihood, drawn through g&rsquo;vuros = the Baal Koach&rsquo;s prayer in Din); why all Yomim Tovim are in remembrance of Y&rsquo;tzias Mitzrayim (the Exodus was accomplished through this same prayer); and why ochel nefesh is permitted on Yom Tov (the labor of food-preparation on Yom Tov IS the prayer of Din — and the Sitra Achara has no foothold in it whatsoever, because it is the very mechanism that extracts its swallowing).

The Bil&rsquo;am episode seals the teaching: Bil&rsquo;am drew Daas into animality itself (literally), but through the Three R&rsquo;galim — the prayer of the Baal Koach — even the donkey vomited up the Daas, speaking and rebuking him. Bil&rsquo;am was forced to turn his curses into blessings: &ldquo;the wealth he swallowed he shall vomit up.&rdquo;

&#10042; Na Nach Nachma Nachman MayUman &#10042;
